Sarcopenia
Geriatric & Frailty
Loss of skeletal muscle mass, Reduced handgrip strength, Slowed gait speed

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GEF-042
Reason for Visit: Acute inability to rise from a chair independently and a recent fall due to severe, progressive leg weakness.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and neighbor
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a frail patient who experienced a non-injurious fall and is now stranded in an armchair, completely unable to stand up due to profound weakness. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to rule out an occult hip fracture, acute spinal cord pathology, or severe electrolyte derangement, and to safely mobilize the patient prior to the next in-hours period to prevent pressure injuries.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My legs have completely lost all their power; I can't even push myself up out of this chair anymore."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a progressive, generalized loss of skeletal muscle mass over the past year, primarily affecting the proximal limbs. Today, this culminated in a severe physical functional impairment leading to an absolute inability to rise from a chair independently. The patient experienced a unprovoked fall earlier today when their legs "simply gave out." They note a significant reduction in muscle strength characterized by a weak handgrip, making it difficult to open jars or hold a walking frame securely. They report a significantly slowed gait speed recently, describing it as "wading through mud." The patient complains of generalized severe fatigue and poor physical endurance. Their dietary history reveals inadequate dietary protein intake (tea and toast diet) and chronic malnutrition. They admit to a complete lack of regular resistance exercise.
Functional Status: Acutely chair-bound; severely restricted mobility requiring maximal assistance for transfers.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of recurrent, injurious falls; high risk of pressure necrosis and caregiver (neighbor) burnout.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/75 | HR: 82 | Temp: 36.4 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 55 Kg
General: Frail, thin, appearing chronically exhausted, sitting slumped in an armchair.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal exam reveals noticeable, visible muscle wasting (loss of skeletal muscle mass), particularly around the shoulders (deltoids) and thighs (quadriceps). Bedside testing confirms a profoundly reduced handgrip strength bilaterally. The patient is completely unable to perform a sit-to-stand maneuver without maximal physical hoist assistance. Once standing, they exhibit a significantly slowed gait speed. There is no bony point tenderness over the hips, spine, or pelvis to suggest an acute fracture from the fall. Neurological exam reveals symmetrical weakness but normal reflexes, intact sensation, and no upper motor neuron signs. (Objective documentation of reduced appendicular skeletal muscle mass utilizing DEXA is an anticipated diagnostic tetrad finding).
Environmental Observations: The home lacks supportive grab rails in the living area; the chair is excessively low, making standing mechanically difficult.
Medication Reconciliation: No recent addition of muscle-wasting medications (e.g., high-dose corticosteroids); taking regular antihypertensives.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Sarcopenia with acute functional decompensation - Status: High Risk
Differentials:

Acute Occult Fracture (e.g., Neck of Femur) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of localized groin or hip pain on palpation, no pain on passive internal rotation, and symmetrical, bilateral nature of the weakness.

Myasthenia Gravis / Neuromuscular Junction Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The profound, visible muscle wasting and the lack of classic fluctuating, fatigable weakness involving the extraocular muscles (ptosis/diplopia) or bulbar muscles.

Acute Severe Hypokalemia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: While possible in malnutrition, the chronic, progressive history of visible muscle loss strongly points to structural sarcopenia rather than a pure, acute metabolic derangement (though urgent bloods are required).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Acute mobility failure)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute functional immobility driven by profound, end-stage skeletal muscle loss (sarcopenia) and malnutrition. Telephone triage accurately identified the immediate danger of the patient being stranded in a chair post-fall. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have resulted in an unassisted overnight stay in a chair, leading to severe pressure injuries, dehydration, and likely a subsequent traumatic fall if they attempted to mobilize to the bathroom. This urgent after-hours intervention allowed for the safe, physical exclusion of an acute fracture, the safe mechanical transfer of the patient to a secure bed, and the immediate orchestration of crucial nutritional and allied health support to reverse the functional collapse.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Assisted the patient safely from the low armchair to their bed using proper manual handling techniques. Prescribed a high-protein oral nutritional supplement (e.g., Ensure Protein) to be taken twice daily immediately. Ordered urgent baseline blood tests (FBC, CMP, Calcium, Magnesium, Vitamin D) via the GP to rule out concurrent metabolic weakness.
Positive Behavioral Change: Implement a structured "protein and push" routine. Step 1: The patient must consume one high-protein supplement immediately after performing five assisted, seated leg extensions every morning and afternoon. Step 2: Upon successful completion of the exercise and protein intake, the neighbor provides positive reinforcement by delivering the patient's daily newspaper and chatting for 10 minutes. Cause-and-effect: Pairing the necessary mechanical muscle stimulus (leg extensions) directly with the required biological building blocks (protein), followed immediately by a highly valued social reward, ensures compliance and directly targets the physical reversal of the sarcopenia.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to the Aged Care Assessment Team (ACAT), a community dietitian, and a physiotherapist.
DME/Safety (Optional): Advised the immediate procurement of a raised armchair or cushion to mechanically assist sit-to-stand transfers.
Follow-up: Routine GP review required within 48-72 hours to review blood tests and coordinate the multidisciplinary rehabilitation plan.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transport to the Emergency Department if the patient experiences another fall resulting in severe pain, inability to bear any weight, head trauma, or if they become completely unable to swallow the nutritional supplements.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: No bones were broken in your fall today, but your leg muscles have wasted away to a critical level, causing the severe weakness.
Clinical Course: Muscle rebuilding in older age is slow. You will not regain your strength overnight. It requires strict adherence to the new protein drinks and daily targeted exercises provided by a physiotherapist.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call an ambulance immediately if you fall again and hit your head, or if you feel severe pain in your hip or groin. You must see your GP next week to organize a physiotherapist to come to your home.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Because you have not been eating enough protein or exercising, your body has slowly consumed its own leg muscles for energy. This is called sarcopenia, and it is why you can no longer push yourself out of the chair. We must immediately start feeding those muscles with high-protein drinks and waking them up with gentle exercises.
Actionable Steps: Drink the high-protein supplements twice a day without fail. Practice your seated leg extensions before drinking the supplement. Do not attempt to stand up from low chairs without assistance until your strength improves.

SMS INFORMATION

Diagnosis: Severe Sarcopenia. No acute fracture. High-protein supplements commenced. Assisted transfers required. Physio/Dietitian referral needed. GP review 48-72h.

